in January 2006 the new horizons space  probe was launched its mission to study  Pluto and its moons after a decade of  travel on 14th of July it will arrive  for a brief encounter a flyby that will  be over in a matter of hours the real  excitement will start on Sunday the 12th  of July as New Horizons approaches Pluto  the onboard camera will photograph both  Pluto and its biggest moon Charon  beaming the images back to earth on  Monday the 13th NASA will receive a  final set of pictures before the flyby  New Horizons is traveling so fast that  even colliding with the dust particles  around Pluto could prove fatal these  images make sure that we will have at  least some close-up images of Pluto  before the most difficult phase of the  mission begins at midnight New Horizons  will then turn its radio antenna away  from Earth and focus its attention on  Pluto and Charon cutting our contact for  nearly 24 hours from this point New  Horizons is on its own at 7:40 9 a.m. on  Tuesday New Horizons will pass just  12,000 kilometers from Pluto its closest  approach and its best chance to take  detailed photos and readings of Pluto's  surface but it has to work quickly as  the tiny probe will whiz by Pluto at  almost 50,000 kilometers an hour just 14  minutes after it passes Pluto this  spacecraft will arrive at Sharon Sharon  is half the size of Pluto and may be  hiding secrets of its own  at 851 a.m. the capsule will pass into  Pluto's shadow as sunlight shines  through Pluto's atmosphere  New Horizons will use its onboard  instruments to investigate the chemical  signature of the gases then an hour and  a half later  New Horizons will pass into Sharon's  shadow scientists aren't expecting  Charon to have an atmosphere but if it  does this will be our best chance to  observe it once the flyby is complete  after almost a day of radio silence  New Horizons can finally turn its  antenna if all goes well  New Horizons will start beaming its data  back to earth slowly its radio antenna  uses less power than a standard light  bulb to send information through five  billion kilometres of space we'll have  to wait in agonizing 16 months for the  transfer to complete  over a decade after its launch New  Horizons is ready to complete its  mission and reveal Pluto's long kept  secrets
 

